[Event "Polerio Defence: the defence itself"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/NMtH8AT9/4H2funNl"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today we learn the Polerio. The calm way to answer the scary fried liver attack. } 1. e4 { He grabs the middle with his king pawn. Meet him nose to nose: pawn to e5. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 { Straight back at him, toe to toe in the centre — that is how every line in this study starts. } (1... c5 { The Sicilian, a great fight in its own right, but a whole different world from the Polerio. } 2. Nf3 d6 { Another day, another study. Today we walk straight at him instead. }) (1... e6 { The French: solid, and also not today's plan. } 2. d4 d5 { A slow central squeeze. We want the open, sharp game instead. }) 2. Nf3 { He pokes your pawn on e5. Defend it and develop in the same breath: knight to c6. [%cal Yb8c6] } 2... Nc6 { You defend the pawn and bring a piece out in the same move — always a good trade. } (2... Nf6 { The Petroff: very solid, very safe, and very much not our study. } 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 { Level and quiet chess. We want the sharp fight instead. }) (2... d6 { The Philidor holds up fine, but it shuts your own dark bishop in behind the pawn. } 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 { He owns the whole centre now, and your pieces are squashed behind that pawn. }) 3. Bc4 { His bishop is already glaring at your pawn on f7. Answer boldly: knight to f6. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg8f6] } 3... Nf6 { The two knights defence. You are daring him to jump the knight into g5. [%cal Gf6e4] } (3... Bc5 { The quiet Italian — just as sound a choice, honestly. } 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 { A calm positional game, with no fried liver ideas to defuse. }) (3... d6 { Too meek here. Your dark-squared bishop is boxed in behind that pawn for a long while. } 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 { He takes over the centre for free while your pieces are still at home. }) (3... Be7 { Safe, but a little sleepy — it gives up the fight for the centre before it starts. } 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 { He sits comfortably in the middle while you are still just waiting. }) 4. Ng5 { There it is — the knight lunges straight at f7. Do not panic: hit back in the centre with d5. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yd7d5] } 4... d5 { Right back at the centre — this is the whole point of the Polerio, hitting his bishop before he can hit you. } (4... Na5 { The right idea, one whole move too soon — the knight had to hit d5 first. } 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. d4 d6 { He grabs the pawn on f7 with check first, and your king is stranded in the open with it gone. }) (4... Bc5 { The Traxler! Wild and famous, and it does not quite hold up against calm defence. } 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. Bc4 Qe8 { You get all the fun of the attack, but he ends up with the better position. }) (4... Nxe4 { Grabbing the e4 pawn while your own f7 is on fire. } 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. d4 h6 { Material is level after the swap, but your king is stuck on e7 and may never get to castle. }) (4... Qe7 { She looks like she is defending f7, but the knight on g5 is aimed at that square too — try to recapture there and you simply lose her. } 5. Bxf7+ Kd8 6. Bb3 { Castling is gone for good, and with his pieces still pouring forward you are simply worse. }) 5. exd5 { Here is the big one. Everyone else takes back on d5 and gets fried by the knight on g5. You will not. [%cal Yc6a5,Ga5c4] } 5... Na5 { The Polerio. Forget the pawn for now, and bite the bishop instead. [%cal Ga5c4] } (5... Nxd5 { This is the fried liver, and it starts right here. } 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. Qf3+ Ke6 8. Nc3 { You come out a piece for a pawn ahead, but your king is stuck in the centre — survivable with exact defence. Would you enjoy defending that over the board? }) (5... Nd4 { The Fritz: sharp, fully playable, and a whole book of theory on its own. } 6. c3 b5 7. Bf1 Nxd5 { Messy fun either way. Our line keeps things a good deal cleaner. }) (5... b5 { The Ulvestad: also completely sound, and also a lot to remember. } 6. Bf1 Qxd5 7. Nc3 Qc5 { Playable, though material is level rather than in your favour, and your queen has to keep dodging his developing pieces. }) (5... Nb4 { It eyes the pawn on c2, but that pawn simply runs past it. } 6. d6 Nbd5 7. dxc7 Qxc7 { He has untangled completely, and you have nothing at all to show for the detour. }) (5... Ne7 { Too passive — now the pawn on f7 has no help at all. } 6. d4 h6 7. Nxf7 Kxf7 8. d6+ { The knight's fork on f7 lands, and now the pawn even arrives with an extra check behind it. }) 6. Bb5+ { Check. He saves the bishop and hits you at the same time. Block with the c pawn. [%cal Yc7c6] } 6... c6 { The pawn blocks the check and keeps every other piece exactly where it belongs. } (6... Bd7 { Blocking with the bishop is playable, just a good deal less testing for him. } 7. Qe2 Be7 8. Nf3 Nxd5 { Fine for you — the knight recaptures the pawn and material is level, though the bishop on d7 is a passive way to spend the move. }) (6... Nd7 { The knight blocks the check, but it leaves a hole on e6 behind it. } 7. Ne6 fxe6 8. dxe6 { His pawn rips your king side open, and there is no tidy way to close it back up. }) (6... Nc6 { The knight retreats to block, but it lands right back on the square your own pawn already attacks. } 7. dxc6 b6 { Gone for nothing — the pawn simply takes the knight the moment it blocks there, and you are down a whole piece. }) (6... Ke7 { Never walk your own king into the middle of the board like that. } 7. O-O Qxd5 8. Nc3 { His pieces pour forward at your naked king, and there is no time left to tidy up. }) 7. dxc6 { He takes the pawn. Which pawn takes back? The b pawn, opening a column for your rook. [%cal Yb7c6] } 7... bxc6 { The b pawn takes back, and your rook on a8 gets a whole open column to dream about. } (7... Nxc6 { Playable, but the pawn was the better piece to recapture with here. } 8. d3 Bc5 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Same pawns in the end, but you handed him a free trade along the way. }) (7... Bd7 { Careful — that pawn on c6 is still moving forward. } 8. cxd7+ Nxd7 9. Qg4 { His pawn grabs the bishop first, and even after you recapture he is a piece ahead and already eyeing g7 too. }) 8. Be2 { The bishop crawls back to e2. Now the key move of the whole line: kick that knight! [%cal Yh7h6] } 8... h6 { Yes! Never let that knight sit comfortably on g5. Now it has to go all the way home. [%csl Rg5] } (8... Bd6 { Natural enough, but the knight on g5 is still glaring at your pawn on f7. } 9. d3 Nd5 10. O-O O-O { Playable, though kicking the knight first would have been the sharper choice. }) (8... Be7 { Sensible, just one move too slow to be the best try. } 9. d3 O-O 10. O-O Rb8 { He is comfortable, and that knight still sits untouched on g5. }) (8... Nd5 { Your knight goes hunting for outposts, but his on g5 is not going anywhere either. } 9. d3 Nf4 10. Bxf4 exf4 { You end up trading off your own best attacking piece for very little. }) (8... Bf5 { A good square for the bishop, just not the most urgent move right now. } 9. d3 h6 10. Nf3 Qc7 { You play h6 anyway, just a whole tempo later than in the main line. }) 9. Nf3 { Home he goes. Chase him again: push the e pawn and win a tempo doing it. [%cal Ye5e4] } 9... e4 { Lovely. The pawn kicks him a second time and grabs real space in the centre. [%cal Ge4f3] } (9... Bd6 { Fine, but the pawn push to e4 was a completely free tempo you left on the table. } 10. d3 O-O 11. O-O Bf5 { Comfortable enough for him now, with nothing forcing him to react. }) (9... Qc7 { A safe square for the queen, but a slow way to spend the move. } 10. d3 Bf5 11. O-O Rd8 { He gets to untangle his whole position in complete peace. }) (9... Bc5 { Active, but it lets him settle his knight and finish developing undisturbed. } 10. d3 O-O 11. O-O Re8 { The pawn push to e4 was the real punch you were missing. }) 10. Ne5 { The knight lands on e5. Challenge it at once: bishop to d6. [%cal Yf8d6] } 10... Bd6 { Perfect. A pawn down, every piece of yours is out and working, and he is still untangling his own. [%cal Gd6e5] } (10... Bc5 { Just as good — this bishop eyes f2 instead of d6. } 11. O-O Qd6 12. d4 Qxd4 { Equal material and a lively game. Our bishop simply points at his knight instead. }) (10... Qd4 { Greedy — the queen goes hunting for the knight far too early. } 11. f4 Bc5 12. Rf1 { He gains a free move harassing her while he finishes building his position. }) (10... Qc7 { Solid, and it still keeps an eye on the knight on e5. } 11. d4 exd3 12. Nxd3 { Also fine, though the bishop does that same job a little better from d6. }) (10... Be6 { Sensible development, though the bishop really belongs on d6 in this line. } 11. d4 Bd6 12. Nc3 { You get there in the end, just a whole tempo poorer for the detour. }) 0-1


[Event "Polerio Defence: he takes with the bishop"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/NMtH8AT9/uU3Cjubj"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3pp1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3pp1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ In the Polerio your pawn sits on d5. Watch closely which piece he takes it back with — the choice changes everything. } 5. Bxd5 { His bishop grabs the pawn on d5 and looks perfectly safe there. Is it really? } 5... Nxd5 { Yes, take with the knight. His bishop was the only piece guarding the knight on g5, and now that guard is gone. [%csl Rg5] } (5... Qxd5 { Look again before you play this: his pawn on e4 simply captures your queen on d5 for free. [%cal Re4d5] }) (5... Bg4 { The bishop looks pretty on g4, but you are simply a pawn down with nothing yet to show for it. } 6. Bxf7+ Ke7 7. f3 h6 8. fxg4 hxg5 { By now he owns two clean pawns, and your king on e7 has nowhere safe left to hide. }) (5... Bc5 { The bishop lunges to c5, aggressive but ignoring that his knight just grabbed your pawn on d5. } 6. Nxf7 Bxf2+ 7. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 8. Bxe4 Qd4+ 9. Kf1 { Wild, but count the wreckage: his knight on f7 still forks the rook in the corner, and by the end he is two whole pieces ahead. }) (5... Nd4 { The knight jumps to d4, but f7 is still the square he really wants to invade. } 6. Bxf7+ Ke7 7. Bc4 b5 { Two pawns down, and your king is still stuck on e7 instead of safely castled. }) 6. exd5 { He recaptures on d5 with the pawn. One of his pieces is now hanging — which one? } 6... Qxg5 { Beautiful, the knight on g5 was left completely undefended, and your queen simply takes it. } (6... Qxd5 { The queen grabs the pawn on d5, but that leaves his knight on g5 alive and well. } 7. O-O h6 8. Nc3 Qd6 { A whole knight sat hanging on g5 the entire time, but this queen retreat ignores it — taking it outright was the real prize. }) (6... Nd4 { The knight goes hunting on d4, while the free piece on g5 just sits there untouched. } 7. d3 Qxd5 8. O-O h6 { By the time you circle back for it, he has tucked his king away and untangled everything. }) (6... Nb4 { The knight chases pawns on b4, while his own knight keeps sitting loose on g5. } 7. d3 Be7 8. c3 Nxd5 { You are still comfortably ahead here, though grabbing the loose knight on g5 outright would have won even more. }) 7. dxc6 { His pawn hits your knight straight back. Careful, what matters more here, the pawn or the piece? } 7... Qxg2 { Exactly, ignore the pawn on g2 for a moment. His rook in the corner is the real target now. [%csl Rh1][%tail] } (7... bxc6 { Recapturing with the pawn lets the whole win slip away — the knight and the rook both survive. } 8. Qf3 Qf6 9. Qxf6 gxf6 { Queens come off and the game goes dead level — the rook in the corner was always the real prize you gave up. }) (7... e4 { Pushing the pawn to e4 is solid, but slow, and it gives him time to untangle his pieces. } 8. O-O Bh3 9. g3 O-O-O { You are still the one better placed, but the forced win from grabbing the rook has slipped away. }) (7... Qf6 { The queen retreat to f6 is safe, but far too modest for this position. } 8. O-O Bc5 9. Qe2 O-O { You are only a little better here — the pawn on g2 and the rook in the corner were both there for free. }) 8. Rf1 8... Bh3 { The rook on f1 is trapped for good — h1 and g1 are both covered, so your queen or your bishop collects it wherever it runs. [%cal Gh3f1] } 0-1


[Event "Polerio Defence: he grabs on f7 anyway"]
[Result "0-1"]
