[Role "exercise"]

{ Deep in the Polerio, your knight has just hit his bishop. Watch how he answers it. } 6. Nxf7 { The knight crashes into f7 and forks your queen and rook. Is the fork actually real? } 6... Kxf7 { The fork was real, but grabbing it was his mistake — that knight has no defender at all, so your king simply eats it, a piece for a pawn in your favour. [%csl Gf7][%tail] } (6... Nxc4 { You grab the bishop, so he grabs your queen in return. } 7. Nxd8 Kxd8 8. d3 Nb6 { His fork on d8 was very real: he nets your whole queen for a bishop and a knight, a huge material swing his way. }) (6... Qe7 { Saving the queen this way lets the knight simply stroll off with your rook instead. } 7. Nxh8 Nxc4 8. O-O Bf5 { He ends up an exchange and two pawns to the good. }) (6... Bg4 { The counter attack looks clever, but his knight grabs your queen first. } 7. Nxd8 Bxd1 8. Nxb7 Nxc4 9. Kxd1 { Queens vanish in the scramble, his knight scoops the pawn on b7 too, and once the smoke clears he is three pawns ahead. }) (6... Qd7 { The queen steps aside, and his knight grabs the pawn on e5 instead. } 7. Nxe5 Qe7 8. O-O Qxe5 { You win the knight straight back and material is level again, but your queen and king now share the e file — not a comfortable place for her to stand. }) 7. Be2 7... c6 8. dxc6 8... Nxc6 { Perfect. That knight, stuck on the rim all game, finally lands on its best square — and he is still down a whole piece for only two pawns. [%csl Gc6] } 0-1


