[Role "exercise"]

{ Same Polerio position again, but this time he props up the bishop on c4 with a pawn instead of giving the check. } 6. d3 { His pawn move defends the bishop on c4. What is the one move you always want here? } 6... h6 { Right, before anything else, kick that knight off g5 with your pawn. [%csl Rg5] } (6... Nxc4 { Trading on c4 is fine, but the real problem is still the knight sitting on g5. } 7. dxc4 h6 8. Nf3 Bd6 { You end up playing h6 anyway, just a move later than you needed to. }) (6... c6 { Chipping at the pawn on d5 is fine in general, but the knight on g5 still needs kicking first. } 7. dxc6 Nxc4 8. dxc4 Qxd1+ { Check! Your queen has just captured his on d1, and his king has no way to avoid recapturing it right back. }) (6... a6 { The pawn move to a6 is useful eventually, but it is not the urgent task in this position. } 7. Qe2 Bd6 8. Bd2 Nxc4 { Your knight grabs the bishop on c4, but his pawn on d3 takes it straight back — kicking the knight on g5 first was the move that actually wins something. }) (6... Bc5 { Developing the bishop to c5 is natural, but a knight is still sitting loose on g5. } 7. Nc3 O-O 8. O-O Nxc4 { The knight helps itself to the bishop on c4, but his pawn takes it straight back — you never got the free kick on g5 you were after. }) 7. Nf3 { Out the knight goes to f3. Now hit it again and grab the centre with your pawn. } 7... e4 { Yes, the pawn to e4 kicks the knight a second time and claims real space. [%cal Ge4f3] } (7... Nxc4 { Trading on c4 is fine, but pushing the pawn to e4 was the bigger idea here. } 8. dxc4 Bd6 9. Be3 Qe7 { You let him breathe here — the trade lets him untangle, and he ends up a touch more comfortable than in the main line. }) (7... a6 { The pawn move to a6 waits again — the moment to hit the knight was right now. } 8. Nxe5 Bd6 9. d4 Nxc4 { His knight grabs the pawn back on e5, and though your knight wins the bishop on c4 in exchange, his other knight simply recaptures it there too — the position settles back to about where it always sits in this line. }) (7... Bd6 { Developing the bishop to d6 is sensible, but too slow for this position. } 8. Bb5+ c6 9. dxc6 Nxc6 { He wriggles free with the check, and by the end he is a little more comfortable than you are. }) (7... Bc5 { The bishop to c5 is active, but it lets his knight grab the pawn on e5. } 8. Nxe5 O-O 9. O-O Nxd5 { A pawn down, and you do not have very much to show for it. }) 8. Nd4 { His knight hops into the middle on d4. Now develop a piece with real punch. } 8... Bc5 { Perfect, the bishop hits the knight on d4 and squints straight at f2 as well. [%cal Gc5d4,Gc5f2] } (8... c6 { Playing c6 first is also completely fine, it just leaves the bishop's best diagonal for later. } 9. O-O Be7 10. Nc3 O-O { Level and perfectly playable, though the bishop is livelier on c5 than it ever is here. }) (8... a6 { Solid, and dead equal, though it does nothing to pressure his knight on d4. } 9. O-O b5 10. Bb3 c5 { Nothing wrong with it at all, just a slower way to reach the same kind of position. }) (8... Bg4 { The pin on g4 is tempting, but his check on b5 comes first and spoils the point. } 9. Bb5+ c6 10. dxc6 Bb4+ { Very messy, and he is not worse for it — the complications favour him just as much as you. }) 0-1


