[Role "exercise"]

{ In the Polerio you blocked his check with the pawn to c6. Now he refuses the trade and tries to hang on to his extra pawn on d5. } 7. Be2 { His bishop tucks away to e2, keeping the extra pawn on d5 for now. Punish that greed. } 7... cxd5 { Yes, take the pawn back with your own, and look at that strong pawn pair you now have in the middle. [%csl Gd5,Ge5] } (7... h6 { The usual kick on the knight, but the pawn on d5 was hanging right now and needed taking first. } 8. Nf3 e4 9. Ne5 Bd6 { Good enough, though he still owns the pawn on d5 that you could have taken back at once. }) (7... Nxd5 { The knight recaptures on d5, but it will only get chased straight back off that square. } 8. d3 c5 9. Ne4 Be7 { The pawn was the stronger piece to take back with — this knight recapture gives back some of your edge. }) (7... a6 { A waiting move, and the pawn on d5 is still there hanging the whole time. } 8. d4 exd4 9. O-O Bd6 { He opens the centre and you have gained nothing beyond what the direct recapture already gave you. }) (7... Be7 { Calm developing, but the pawn on d5 was there for free and this move lets it go. } 8. dxc6 h6 9. Nf3 Nxc6 { You are back to the usual single pawn deficit — the direct recapture on d5 would have kept things dead level instead. }) 8. d4 { His pawn strikes back in the centre at once. Do not trade, push your own pawn straight past it. } 8... e4 { Exactly, now your pawns roll forward together, and his knight on g5 is stranded with nowhere useful to go. [%csl Rg5] } (8... exd4 { Trading pawns on d4 only helps him untangle his position. } 9. Nf3 Bc5 10. Nxd4 O-O { Equal chess here too — pushing straight past him instead would have kept the bind tighter still. }) (8... Bb4+ { A check on b4 that solves nothing — he simply blocks and carries on. } 9. c3 Bd6 10. dxe5 Bxe5 { He straightens out his pawns, and the position settles back to level. }) (8... Nc6 { The knight comes back to c6, but it lets him trade off the tension in the centre. } 9. dxe5 Nxe5 10. O-O h6 { Balanced chess, though pushing the pawn straight past him kept more of your edge. }) (8... Bd6 { Developing the bishop to d6 is natural, but one beat too late to stop the trade. } 9. dxe5 Bxe5 10. Nf3 Bd6 { Dead equal, and the big pawn centre you were building has gone with it. }) 9. O-O { He castles his king out of trouble. Finish your own development on the bishop's best square. } 9... Bd6 { Lovely, two big pawns stand side by side in the middle, and he is still the one tangled up trying to finish developing. [%csl Gd5,Ge4] } (9... h6 { Also strong, the same knight-kick as before, just played a move sooner. } 10. Nh3 Bd6 11. f3 Bxh3 { The knight on h3 gets swallowed the same way, and you come out clearly better whichever move order you pick. }) (9... Nc6 { The knight to c6 is good too, just a touch slower to reach the same position. } 10. f3 h6 11. Nh3 Bxh3 { Still very pleasant for you, the knight on h3 falls the same way as before. }) (9... Be7 { Passive, the bishop belongs on d6, biting toward f2, not tucked away on e7. } 10. f3 h6 11. Nh3 Bxh3 { Fine either way, but d6 was always the sharper square for this bishop. }) 0-1


