[Role "exercise"]

{ Deep in the Polerio, your pawn has just recaptured on c6, and his bishop on b5 is hanging to it. Watch which square he picks to save it. } 8. Qf3 { The queen jumps out to f3, eyeing your pawn on c6 from a distance. The bishop on b5 is still hanging though, so stay calm and keep developing. } 8... Be7 { Right. The bishop comes out and clears the road to castle — his queen's diagonal is blocked at c6, so your rook in the corner was never really in danger. } (8... cxb5 { Very sharp — your pawn grabs the hanging bishop on b5, and the whole line still holds up for you. } 9. Qxa8 Qc7 10. Qf3 Nc6 { You are down the exchange and a pawn now, but his queen burned three whole moves doing it, and the position stays dead level. }) (8... Rb8 { Sensible enough, but it spends a move on a rook nothing was really attacking, while the bishop on b5 just retreats to safety anyway. } 9. Be2 Be7 10. Ne4 Nxe4 { Fine, just a touch less tidy than developing the bishop first. }) (8... h6 { The usual kick on the knight, but his queen on f3 already has plans of her own — this fight is not settled yet. } 9. Ne4 Nd5 10. Ba4 Be7 { Playable, and he keeps a small pull — the bishop tucks safely onto a4 and eyes your queenside. }) (8... Bb4 { Bb4 looks active, but nothing stands behind it, and next move he simply kicks the bishop back with a3. } 9. Bd3 O-O 10. a3 Be7 { Two whole moves spent on that bishop, and it ends up back on e7 with nothing at all to show for the trip. }) 9. Bd3 { The bishop steps back to d3, aiming down the diagonal toward h7 on your kingside. Get your own king out of the middle before that diagonal matters. } 9... O-O { Right. Your king tucks away safe, the rook joins the game on f8, and you can finally breathe. } (9... Rb8 { The rook finds a useful file, but your king is still stuck in the centre, and that matters more right now. } 10. b3 O-O 11. Nc3 Rb4 { Fine — you castle next move regardless, so nothing was really lost by the detour. }) (9... h6 { The knight on g5 is not your real problem — his queen and bishop are already lined up toward your king. } 10. Ne4 Nd5 11. Ng3 g6 { Playable, but g6 leaves real holes around your king that he can try to use later. }) (9... g6 { It rules out any Qh5 and bishop tricks on your king, but the dark squares around it get a little loose in return. } 10. Nc3 h6 11. Nge4 Nd5 { Playable — your king has a touch less cover than in the main line, but nothing concrete is wrong yet. }) 10. Nc3 { He brings his last piece out. Now play the little pawn move you have been saving. } 10... h6 { Yes. That knight on g5 has nowhere left to hide and must finally leave for good. [%csl Rg5] } (10... Rb8 { A good square for the rook, but the knight on g5 is still sitting there untouched. } 11. b3 h6 12. Nge4 Nd5 { Same idea, just a tempo slower — you still had to kick that knight off g5 with h6 in the end. }) (10... Nb7 { Bringing the knight home from a5 is the right idea eventually, just not yet. } 11. O-O h6 12. Nge4 Nd5 { Kick the knight on g5 first — that was the more urgent problem, and the rerouted knight can wait its turn. }) (10... Bb7 { The bishop on b7 stares at a wall of pawns and bites on granite there. } 11. b4 h6 12. bxa5 hxg5 { He grabs the knight on a5 with his pawn, you take his knight on g5 straight back, and the pawns start flying on both wings. }) 11. Nge4 { He offers a trade with the knight from g5. Meet him in the middle, on your best square. } 11... Nd5 { Perfect. The knight lands on d5 in the very centre, and the position is dead level. [%csl Gd5] } (11... Nxe4 { Trading knights is fine in itself, but it lets his bishop recapture toward the centre with extra reach. } 12. Bxe4 f5 13. Bxc6 Nxc6 { Equal on the scoreboard, though your position is a shade looser than in the main line. }) (11... Nh7 { The knight on f6 retreats to h7 for no real reason, giving up on the centre. } 12. Ng3 Ng5 13. Qh5 Nb7 { Now he is the one with the plan, his queen and knight both eyeing your kingside. }) (11... Ne8 { Too humble — that knight belongs in the middle of the fight, not tucked further away on e8. } 12. Ng3 Nd6 13. Nf5 Bxf5 { You untangle eventually, but it costs time, and he ends up a touch better for it. }) 0-1


