{ The same Polerio crossroads as before, only now watch which square his bishop picks for its retreat. } 8. Bd3 { The bishop retreats to d3, aiming down the diagonal toward h7 on your kingside. Where does your knight belong now? } 8... Nd5 { Nice. From d5 your knight eyes f4, the very square his bishop is standing on. [%cal Gd5f4] } (8... Ng4 { Adventurous, but there is no pawn on h2 yet for the knight to attack, so the jump has no real target. } 9. Ne4 f5 10. Be2 h5 { Very loose — those pawn lunges tear open your own king, and he picks up a small but real pull for it. }) (8... Bd6 { Natural enough, but the knight on f6 had the more useful job of jumping to d5 first. } 9. Nc3 Nd5 10. h4 Nf4 { You reach the very same knight jump a move later than the main line — no harm done, just a slower route. }) (8... Bc5 { Active, but it gives up a tempo by leaving the knight on f6 to develop next instead of jumping straight to d5. } 9. O-O O-O 10. Nc3 h6 { Playable, but he keeps a real, lasting pull here — sharper was grabbing d5 with the knight first. }) (8... h6 { Kicking the knight is usually the first idea, but here your own knight had a square to grab first, on d5. } 9. Ne4 Nxe4 10. Bxe4 Bd6 { That trade removes your best-placed knight and lets his bishop recapture toward the centre instead. }) 9. Nf3 { The knight on g5 retreats to f3 on its own. Keep developing, with f4 still the knight's dream square. } 9... Bd6 { Good. The bishop comes out, castling is next, and the knight on d5 still dreams of landing on f4. } (9... Nb4 { The knight jumps to b4, forking at the bishop on d3 and the pawn on c2 — but he is happy to just trade the bishop off. } 10. O-O Nxd3 11. cxd3 Bd6 { His doubled pawn on d3 is not the weakness it looks — it opens the c file for him and keeps a firm foothold on e4. }) (9... Nf4 { The very same jump to f4, just one move too soon — the bishop still has a square to run to. } 10. Bf1 e4 11. d3 exf3 { You chase the bishop all the way home, but he simply steps aside each time, and he ends up clearly for choice. }) (9... Qc7 { Fine and calm, though it does not ask him anything the way jumping the knight to d5 first does. } 10. O-O Bd6 11. Re1 O-O { You reach the same kind of position as the main line, only a real tempo poorer for the detour. }) 10. O-O { He tucks his king away and castles. Now jump the knight in. } 10... Nf4 { Yes. The knight lands on f4 and attacks the bishop on d3 — he can still run it to safety, but the trade this sets up suits you fine. [%cal Gf4d3] } (10... O-O { Safe enough, but landing the knight on f4 to hit the bishop was the whole point of this line. } 11. Re1 Re8 12. Nc3 f5 { Playable, just a calmer game than the one you get by jumping the knight to f4. }) (10... Qc7 { Slow — the knight on d5 had a job waiting for it on f4. } 11. Re1 O-O 12. c4 Nf4 { You get the knight to f4 eventually anyway, just a couple of moves later than the main line. }) (10... Rb8 { The rook is fine on b8, but jumping the knight to f4 first was the sharper choice. } 11. Re1 O-O 12. Bf1 Re8 { His bishop simply tucks back home to f1, safe from the knight you never got to launch. }) 0-1


