{ In this Polerio branch his bishop is still stuck on b5, and now he decides where to tuck it away. Watch which square he picks. } 8. Bf1 { The bishop retreats the whole way home to f1, giving up nothing but time. His knight on g5 is still out there — what is the move you always play against it? } 8... h6 { Right. The pawn on h6 attacks the knight on g5 head on, so no matter what else he tries next, that knight always has to move first. [%csl Rg5] } (8... Bc5 { Developing the bishop is natural, but it leaves the knight on g5 completely untouched. } 9. Nc3 Qd4 10. Qf3 O-O { He tucks everything into place, and since you never forced the knight on g5 to move, he never lost a tempo over it. }) (8... Nd5 { A good square for the knight, but the knight on g5 was the more urgent target right now. } 9. d3 f5 10. Be2 Bd6 { The pawn to f5 only weakens your own king, since nothing of his was actually attacking it. }) (8... Bf5 { A nice square for the bishop, but the timing is off — the knight on g5 needed kicking first. } 9. d3 h6 10. Nf3 Qc7 { The bishop looks pretty, but you still have to play h6 to move that knight along, just a tempo later than if you had led with it. }) (8... Qb6 { The queen is active on b6, but nothing there needs her yet, and the knight on g5 still stands untouched. } 9. Nc3 Bb4 10. Be2 O-O { White untangles in comfort, since the queen trip did nothing at all about the knight on g5. }) 9. Nh3 { Right where he has to put it — the rim, where the knight on h3 will do nothing for the rest of the game. } 9... Bd6 { Good. The bishop claims its best diagonal while the knight on h3 rots on the edge of the board. [%csl Rh3] } (9... Rb8 { The rook finds the b column, already clear of your own pawns, but the bishop still wants its best diagonal first. } 10. Be2 Bd6 11. d3 c5 { You reach the very same set-up, just with the rook move slotted in earlier instead of later. }) (9... Bc5 { This bishop diagonal is genuinely fine too, though d6 aims more directly at the centre than c5 does. } 10. Nc3 Nb7 11. Be2 Bxh3 { The bishop scoops the knight on h3, and for the moment you are up a whole piece — his pawn on g2 is the only thing that can answer it, and d6 always suited that bishop better anyway. }) (9... Nb7 { The right idea for that knight, just a touch early — the bishop still wants to reach d6 first. } 10. d3 Bc5 11. Nc3 O-O { You are still perfectly comfortable, just a tempo slower than sending the bishop to d6 straight away. }) (9... Bxh3 { You can grab the knight right now, but hold off — that bishop is worth more than the knight sitting on the rim. } 10. gxh3 Bc5 11. Bg2 O-O { White's bishop lands happily on g2, and the trade cost you your better piece for his worse one. }) 10. Be2 { The bishop finally steps out, and now he is ready to tuck his king away next. } 10... O-O { Perfect. Your king is safe, every piece already has a job, and his knight is still doing nothing at all on h3. } (10... e4 { Grabbing more space with the pawn works just as well right here. } 11. d3 O-O 12. Nf4 Qc7 { This push works too, but tucking your king away first leaves him nothing at all to grab onto. }) (10... Rb8 { The rook finds the b column, already clear of your own pawns, but your king still comes first. } 11. O-O c5 12. d3 O-O { You reach the very same position, since you castle on the very next move anyway. }) (10... Bc7 { Stepping the bishop back does nothing useful — d6 was already its best square. } 11. Nc3 O-O 12. d3 c5 { Still playable, but the bishop retreat wastes a move that castling could have used instead. }) 0-1


