{ In this Polerio line you have already kicked the knight with your pawn. Watch: does he send it home the normal way, or hide it somewhere stranger? } 9. Nh3 { To h3, not the usual f3 — he keeps the knight alive, but tucked on the rim where it will barely touch the game. [%csl Rh3] } 9... Bc5 { Good. The bishop claims the diagonal toward f2 while his knight rots uselessly on the rim. } (9... Nb7 { Also right — the knight on a5 is doing nothing there and needs a fresh square. } 10. O-O Bc5 11. Nc3 O-O { You reach the very same position, just with the bishop and the knight taking their turns in the other order. }) (9... Rb8 { Perfectly good — the rook takes up the b column, already clear of your own pawns, well before you actually need it there. } 10. d3 Bc5 11. O-O O-O { Level chess, and comfortable — every piece has a square, and his knight is still stuck on the rim. }) (9... Bd6 { Just as fine, though the bishop aims more sharply from c5 than it does from d6. } 10. d3 O-O 11. Nc3 Rb8 { Equal chess — the bishop still does useful work from d6, just with a little less bite than it has from c5. }) (9... Bxh3 { You can grab the knight, but think twice — your bishop is worth more than a knight stuck on the rim. } 10. gxh3 Bc5 11. O-O O-O { Equal chess — you gave up that bishop for the knight on the rim, and the position is just as level with fewer pieces around each king. }) (9... g5 { Hunting the knight works, but the pawn to g5 also tears open the cover in front of your own king. } 10. d3 Bg7 11. Ng1 O-O { He tucks the knight safely back to g1, and the holes the pawn push left behind are still there long after. }) 10. O-O { He tucks his king away. One of your pieces has done nothing all game — bring it back to life. Which one is it? } 10... Nb7 { Exactly. The knight on a5 was doing nothing at all, and from b7 it heads straight for d6. [%cal Gb7d6] } (10... O-O { Safe and sensible — the king tucks away before anything else happens. } 11. Nc3 Nb7 12. Kh1 Bxh3 { You castle first and reach the same trade — right now you are up a piece, with only his pawn on g2 left to take it back. }) (10... Bxh3 { Grabbing the knight already works here too, though it is still a touch early — the knight on a5 wants moving first. } 11. gxh3 O-O 12. Bf3 Nc4 { Equal chess, and the knight finally escapes the rim on a5 for the lively square c4. }) (10... Rb8 { A good square for the rook, but the knight stuck on a5 is the piece that really needs help. } 11. d3 O-O 12. Nc3 Nb7 { You bring the knight to b7 anyway, just a move later than sending it there right away. }) 11. Nc3 { He brings his last piece out. Finish the job and tuck your own king away. } 11... O-O { Lovely. Your king is safe, every piece is doing something, and his knight is still stuck doing nothing at all on h3. [%cal Gc8h3] } (11... Bxh3 { Grabbing the knight works here too — his pawn on g2 is the only piece that can answer it. } 12. gxh3 Nd6 13. d3 Nf5 { Equal chess — he keeps the bishop pair for it, but your knights land on lovely squares, and castling first would have kept it all a touch tidier. }) (11... Bb6 { A safe square for the bishop, but your king is still stuck in the middle of the board. } 12. Kh1 Nd6 13. d3 O-O { You castle on the very next move regardless, so nothing here was actually lost. }) (11... Nd6 { The knight reaches its best square, but your king is still stuck at home with nowhere safe to go. } 12. Na4 Bb6 13. Nxb6 Qxb6 { He swaps his knight for your good bishop, an even trade that still costs you the bishop pair. }) 0-1
